the phenotracker project allows you to  get high throughput data from video  monitoring  of animal models of human disease so you  you here you can see a little preview of  what the analysis looks like and if you  scroll down  you'll see a lot more information about  what is being done as well as some of  the history of the project that we  developed  together with ucsf and darpa  now you can also learn more about the  hardware that we use  which is a an insert to the lid with  these modules  and you can see how the modules that are  easily detachable from the lid  could be converted into multiple types  of sensors like  microphones uv ready uv  illuminated camera capturing  and thermal camera as well so you can  see here an example of what the full  cage kind of looks like now if we  go inside the tracker software here i'm  gonna  log in with my google account  so after you log in you'll see that you  have a dashboard of all of the  experiments that are ongoing and you can  kind of  go into a real-time view if you go down  here to the experiment  tab so let's take a look at this first  one  you see here that there are three cages  essentially three  mice here's a principal component  analysis obviously right now with just  three mice  it does not make that much sense but you  can change it to the day view  and you can see how for each one of the  mice you have multiple days  and so this green mouse which is  uh mouse 26 so this one is a little bit  different from the rest right so they're  all kind of cluster right here  and here you can see that starting to go  sideways so now you can also go  into the dashboard and start seeing  what does the data actually look like so  here you have a real-time view  of the cages they consist of three  cameras each  those cameras capture the animal and the  animal is tracked so when you see these  three  dots the largest dot shows the head  and then this is the body and this is  the tail and here you've got  the data so you can see what the data  actually looks like  now you saw that 26 is a little bit  different from the rest  and indeed right away we can see that  okay it's sleeping in the middle  these are sleeping on the side so it  does look a little bit different this  also has high  temperature composed of these compared  to these so there is something going on  now if you go to the dashboard right  here  you can see a real-time view with a heat  map  of where the location is in the past  period of time of just maybe 10 30  minutes  and if you scroll down you will start  seeing  the data so if you scroll  if you kind of enlarge this you can see  the area changes and we can remove that  now you can see the speed  let's remove some of these  okay so here you have speed  size and temperature right so you have  these spikes  in uh temperature for example which is  interesting  now here at the bottom you have a view  this is an automated system to track  different types of activities so for  example slow movement moderate movement  fast movement  you can see that the fast movement does  correlate with temperature but not  perfectly you can see that some of the  temperature is considered to be high  now this could be because of the camera  normalization in the beginning you can  kind of normalize everything if you  place an object with the same  temperature to make sure that it's  capturing the right kind of information  and obviously once you've seen  this visually you would like to analyze  and understand  what are the differences between these  different mice or between different days  so that's why we have here a section  called analysis you can also  export the data that will come in a  table format so you can analyze it on  your computer  or you can perform some analysis right  here including  seasonality decomposition and some other  types of comparisons so in fact if you  want to read more about  what types of analyses are possible you  can see some examples here  this is a time warp difference  between healthy and sick conditions  you can see that some of the analysis is  briefly described here  and importantly you can develop a model  of  condition changes over time so this is a  little preview  of the phenotracker project and  you can learn more by going to  fino.tracker.comtracker is with  trkir.com and learn much more about it
 

